---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Site from '../../layouts/Site.astro';
import Card from '../../components/ui/Card.astro';
import Section from '../../components/ui/Section.astro';
import Eyebrow from '../../components/ui/Eyebrow.astro';

/*
 * The header and footer both link "Compare"; without this index that link has nowhere to go once
 * there is more than one comparison. It lists the collection, so a new .mdx file appears here.
 */
const entries = (await getCollection('compare')).sort((a, b) =>
	a.data.competitor.localeCompare(b.data.competitor)
);
---

<Site
	title="Compare — FoxAuth"
	description="How FoxAuth compares with Keycloak and Auth0 on hosting, protocol coverage, administration and licensing — dated, and sourced from their own documentation."
>
	<Section narrow>
		<Eyebrow>Compare</Eyebrow>
		<h1 class="text-4xl font-[650] leading-[1.15] tracking-tight text-balance">
			Checked against their own documentation.
		</h1>
		<p class="mt-5 text-lg text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
			Each comparison lists the pages we read and the date we read them. Where a product's
			documentation did not answer a question, the row says "not documented" instead of guessing.
		</p>
	</Section>

	<Section narrow tone="muted">
		<Eyebrow>The short version</Eyebrow>
		<h2 class="text-3xl font-[650] tracking-tight text-balance">
			Three questions decide it, and none of them is a feature count.
		</h2>
		<div class="mt-6 flex max-w-3xl flex-col gap-4 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
			<p>
				<strong class="text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">Where does the data live?</strong> This
				is the question that settles most of them. A managed service holds your users on its
				infrastructure and bills you for them; FoxAuth holds them in a database you run and bills
				you for nothing. If a regulator, a contract or a procurement review requires identity
				data to stay in your own estate, the decision is already made and the rest of this page
				is detail.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong class="text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">What does the bill grow with?</strong>{' '}
				Hosted identity is priced per monthly active user, which is cheap while you are small and
				is the reason most teams start looking. Self-hosting moves that cost to infrastructure
				and to the engineering time that keeps a server healthy — a smaller bill at scale, and a
				real one.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong class="text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">
					What does your team already run?
				</strong>{' '}
				A team fluent in the JVM with a relational database has a shorter path to Keycloak than
				to anything else, whatever the protocol tables say. A team writing TypeScript has the
				opposite. Operational familiarity beats a feature comparison more often than anyone
				admits in a comparison table.
			</p>
			<p>
				Where FoxAuth is genuinely behind, the pages say so plainly: it has no SAML support and
				none planned, it is at a <code>0.x</code> release with an HTTP surface that may still
				change between minor versions, and it has none of the production hardening years of
				deployment buys. Where it is ahead — the banking-grade profiles without a plan tier, and
				an AI agent administering the instance through the same audited path a human uses — the
				rows say that too.
			</p>
		</div>
	</Section>

	<Section tone="muted">
		<div class="grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
			{
				entries.map((entry) => (
					<Card href={`/compare/${entry.id}/`}>
						<h2 class="text-lg font-[650] tracking-tight">{entry.data.title}</h2>
						<p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
							{entry.data.description}
						</p>
						<p class="mt-3 text-sm font-medium text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">
							{entry.data.bottomLine}
						</p>
						<p class="mt-4 text-xs text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">
							Last checked {entry.data.lastChecked} · {entry.data.sources.length} sources
						</p>
					</Card>
				))
			}
		</div>
	</Section>
</Site>
